speechtext:
I do^not imagine that the minister will be able to give the information I desire at once, but I would ask him to make note of the case that I call to his attention. I have a communication from a thoroughly responsible correspondent. I know nothing personally of the circumstances, but the facts as stated in this communication are in effect these: In the fall of 1903, Mr. J. L. McDonald was appointed teacher of the Indians at Barra Head or Salmon River, being recommended to the Indian Department by the Indian agent. He conducted the school weil and the Indians had great regard for him. witn-out warning, about the end of March last he received a letter from the Indian agent stating tliat lie had heard bad reports ol him and that he understood these reports had been sent to the Indian Department and that the teacher would undoubtedly be dismissed unless he resigned. The agent advised him to tender his resignation, letter was dated March 23rd, 1905. This was followed two days later by abetter which dismissed him from bis position as teacher. This letter contained some very harsh adjectives in regard to this young man's character and told him to go home and never show his face again m Salmon river where he had created such a scandal. The young man was entirely ignorant of what the offence charged against him was. He became alarmed and sent in his resigna-

tion and desired to know the cause of his dismissal. He has not had any explanation since but, in consequence of his dismissal and of what has been said, reports have been circulated against him which are very prejudicial to his character. It seems that afterwards the agent sent a letter, dated April 3rd, in which he told the young man that he felt that he would be re-instated provided he could get a certificate of good moral character. This he procured. It was forwarded to the agent. But no further notice has been taken of the matter. Another teacher has been appointed. Mr. McDonald, who is a very young man, desired an investigation and still desires it. He does not wish to begin life under the stigma of having been dismissed in disgrace by the Indian Department, especially as he has no idea of the cause of his dismissal. What I would like to know is :	Why Mr. McDonald was dismissed ;
whether there were any charges made against him ; if so, what was the nature of the charges; why he was dismissed without the holding of an investigation [DOT] whether it is or is not true that he was threatened with dismissal before he resigned, and,i generally, all the information to cover the matter as I have referred to it. If the minister will be good enough to have one of his officials make a note of this matter and will give the information on some subsequent occasion when his estmates are before the House, I shall be very glad. I have every confidence that my correspondent, who is a man in very high position*, would not make these representations to me unless he felt absolutely that the facts are as stated. Of courge, it is possible that he may have been deceived ; but so far as he is concerned, I am sure that everything has been stated exactly as he believes the facts to be.	*
